{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1096417-range-of-magick-in-m20-sans-correspondence","id":1096417,"title":"Range of magick in M20 sans correspondence","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-05-24T11:18:31-07:00","id":1096417,"title":"Range of magick in M20 sans correspondence","content":"Recently in the CWoD facebook group someone asked what the range of a spell was without Correspondence. Ordinarily I would have said &quot;line of sight&quot; but another person said &quot;touch&quot; and I looked it up to be sure... and now I am seeing conflicting information.<br /> <br /> On P.504 there is the Correspondence Sphere Ranges chart which shows one success as having the range of &quot;line of sight.&quot;<br /> <br /> Also, on P.513 in the description for the Correspondence sphere it says<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because this sphere deals with space and relationships, its Effects use a special Correspondence Sphere Ranges chart found on P.504. Most Effects require touch or close contact, but Correspondence lets the mage reach across distances and affect hidden or faraway targets. </div> </div> </div>But on P.543...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Range<br /> <br /> As mentioned under the divided successes option (see the optional rules sidebar) a typical Effect affects one target within the caster's clear sensory range. If you choose not to use that option, simply assume that you can affect one target per success if the spell you cast is capable of affecting several targets at once... a sleep spell, for instance, rather than a healing spell. Specifically targeted spells (mind control, curses, transformations, etc.) reach only one target at a time. Area-effect workings -explosions, broadcasts, storms, and so on - reach everyone within the area of that feat.<br /> <br /> A target on the fringes of the mage's sensory range (under cover, far away, obscured by fog or forest) increases the difficulty of the caster's Arete roll by +1. A mage with Correspondence 3 or better can expand her sensory range rather dramatically, as shown on the Correspondence Ranges chart. That mage's familiarity with expanded perceptions allows her to ignore that penalty, all other mages, however, must take it into account.<br /> <br /> If your target is out of sight, behind barriers, or otherwise beyond the normal reach of your senses, then you must use the Correspondence Sphere in order to reach him. A song might affect someone who can't see the singer, for example, but it won't reach someone who cannot hear it unless the singing mage adds a Correspondence Effect to connect the song to its intended target. (For details, see the entry right below this one.) </div> </div> </div>On the next page there is a sidebar describing how to use the Correspondence Sphere Ranges chart but there doesn't seem to be any relevant information for someone without Correspondence.<br /> <br /> So this feels like I am getting conflicting information. What are your thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T11:46:04-07:00","id":1096430,"title":"","content":"M20 is replicating language from M2nd, but 2nd ed spell examples ignore the Correspondence Sphere claim, so I would assume that you should trust pg 543 (or page 165 of Mage2nd or page 151 of MageRev.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i> I attack people with giant insects both </i>on <i>and </i>off <i>the court.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes","name":"Five Eyes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1653_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"395"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T12:10:18-07:00","id":1096438,"title":"","content":"I agree.<br /> <br /> I understand the claim of 1 success of Correspondence for &quot;line of sight&quot; as something that only applies as a requisite to use Correspondence itself, but not as implying that Correspondence it's necessary to cast every spell<br /> <br /> Examples:<br /> <br /> To send the sound of your magical song to someone that can't hear it (as per the example P.543 example) but that it's within your sensory range (maybe in another building, with soundproff glass?)...then you may want to add Correspondence, 1 success would be enough.<br /> <br /> To teleport yourself to someplace that it's in your line of sight, you just need 1 success on the range chart and no other connection. This does make short range teleport easy, which I think it's intended.<br /> <br /> To snatch the contents of the someone's pocket with Correspondence + Matter, you only need 1 success on the Correspondence side of the equation, as long as the guy it's within your line of sight.<br /> <br /> You usually wont need Correspondence to throw a fireball to someone in your line of sight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-24T14:02:30-07:00","id":1096474,"title":"","content":"The 1 success might also apply in situations where you're trying to perform some more complicated Effect at range, but are currently scrying that location. If you use Correspondence 2 to view a distant location or subject, then any Effects you subsequently used on that location or subject would, indeed, be &quot;within line of sight&quot;. You can see it, can't you?<br /> <br /> This interpretation serves to reward a mage for remotely viewing their location or subject of interest, before engaging in more potent magicks. You can roll your successes using a lower difficulty - both because Correspondence 2 has a lower rank than more powerful Effects, and because it's generally coincidental. It doesn't remove completely the need to establish connections, but it makes it less difficult overall, even if it requires more total work. And it subtly trains the mage to avoid casting spells blind, which can cause a great deal of trouble.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T02:22:25-07:00","id":1096658,"title":"","content":"Without Correspondence, spells have a range of the Mage's clear sensory perception - usually called 'like of sight' because, well, we like to use our eyes. But technically all mundane sensory perceptions fall under the category of spells not needing correspondence to work.<br /> And as usual, it all depends on paradigm a little bit.<br /> <br /> A blind mage might as well use their hearing (on top of touch) as their primary way of sensing things a mundane way. So even if you are around a corner, if they hear you, their spell can affect you - they are good at pinpointing with it, and their sensory barriers are not lack of sight, but lack of hearing and touch.<br /> <br /> Likewise, a chorister might send a mind attack through corridors using a loud song that only works when others hear it - again skipping the need for correspondence, and using a more mundane medium to 'carry' the effect to unseen targets. On the other hand, stuff your ears and it stops working. Just like staying behind cover stops an eye-focused mage to reach you easily. That and sound is rather indiscriminate in its targeting. Unless you use forces. Oh the possibilities.<br /> <br /> While it's possible for one to rules-thumb that correspondence is always needed for anything that you can't literally see (for some reason our oculars seem to be super special!), I think including all senses for 'sensory range' makes for much more interesting magic. It's not about seeing. It's about how you are naturally and primarily sensing. There is no reason to single out eyes because eyes, and it stands to reason a blind person is much better at sensing with their hearing than a normal person.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 05-25-2017, 05:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T06:21:46-07:00","id":1096694,"title":"","content":"&quot;The sound made by the Krakatoa volcanic eruption in 1883 was so loud it ruptured eardrums of people 40 miles away, travelled around the world four times, and was clearly heard 3,000 miles away.&quot;<br /> <br /> So who knows, if you really need to immolate someone 3000 miles away, you might just get lucky.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-25T13:48:35-07:00","id":1096853,"title":"","content":"I always tended to assume the inconsistency in the rule book had to do with the type of spell/ritual/effect cast as well as the tool/practice involved. To compare to D&amp;D, shocking grasp, burning hands, and magic missile were all considered first level spells at one point (though I think they aren't in the current edition), but they each have a different target range because they do different things,<br /> <br /> If your technique involves writing runes or sigils, then you're probably going to need to be in touching distance with the runes/sigils directly applied to or next to the target. If you want to stand on one side of the room and apply sigils to an object on the other side of the room, I'd probably rule that you need correspondence even if it's line of sight.<br /> <br /> An Etherite ray gun on the other hand would be good for line of sight, but if you wanted to hit someone that you could hear but not see on the other side of a wall, you'd probably need correspondence. (Unless it's powerful enough to blast the wall on top of them, anyway.)<br /> <br /> If you're mind controlling someone with music, then the guy on the other side of the wall is probably yours for the taking. But you'd need correspondence to get at the lady that you can see on the other side of soundproof glass.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}